WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.840
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, Episode 94 for July 6th, 2026.

00:00:06.680 --> 00:00:10.500
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:14.060
We've got some really cool AI stuff to talk about today.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.200
Let's dive in.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:21.100
Have you ever asked a question in class but kept it small because you were worried it sounded silly?

00:00:21.760 --> 00:00:25.100
That habit might be holding you back with some new AI tools.

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:30.060
Ethan Mollick, who studies how people actually use these systems in real life,

00:00:30.300 --> 00:00:34.560
points out that most of us start too timid when we talk to a model called Fable.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:41.940
Instead of easing in with tiny requests, he suggests leading with the biggest, wildest version of what you want.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:48.440
This approach shows you the outer limits right away so you learn faster what the system can truly handle.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:53.660
Think of it like trying out a new pair of running shoes by sprinting the full track first,

00:00:53.660 --> 00:00:55.560
instead of walking a single lap.

00:00:56.260 --> 00:01:01.720
You discover exactly where the shoes grip well and where they slip, then you adjust with real knowledge.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.320
Another way to picture it is ordering the largest possible pizza on the menu before deciding on toppings.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.420
Once you see how much space you have, scaling back feels easy and informed.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:19.260
The reason this matters is simple, newer models like Fable are getting cheaper to run,

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:22.800
so experimenting with bold ideas costs almost nothing.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:28.600
You waste less time on safe, small prompts that never reveal the model's real strengths.

00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:33.540
For a student working on a history project or a writer sketching story ideas,

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.220
this means you reach useful answers quicker.

00:01:36.860 --> 00:01:39.800
You stop guessing at the edges and start seeing them clearly.

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:46.560
To try it yourself, open whatever chat window hosts Fable and type your biggest possible request first.

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:51.620
Notice where the answer shines and where it falls short, then refine from there.

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:57.080
Okay, now for my favorite part of the show, the Deep Dive, where we look under the hood.

00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:04.940
Researchers at Anthropic created something called JSpace, a way to watch which parts of an AI light up while it thinks.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:14.160
It works a bit like the way your phone suggests the next word when you text a friend, except the AI tracks patterns across its whole structure at once.

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:18.340
Imagine the model as a tall apartment building with many floors.

00:02:18.340 --> 00:02:25.680
Each floor handles one slice of the job, such as figuring out your question or picking the next word in the reply.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:32.000
JSpace adds little glowing labels to the exact floors that stay busy during a conversation,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.000
almost like security cameras that show movement in real time.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:43.240
By watching the pattern of lights, the team can spot when the model is about to wander off track and gently steer it back.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:52.660
The process happens in layers, one after another, so seeing the activity gives a clear map of how the answer forms, instead of leaving everything hidden.

00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:59.320
This feels surprisingly close to how different regions of our own brains seem to manage separate parts of a thought.

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:08.680
The result is a window into the choices the AI makes, turning something that used to feel like a locked box into something you can actually follow step by step.

00:03:08.680 --> 00:03:13.660
That clearer picture helps researchers improve the model without guessing in the dark.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:17.540
Now let's move to some hands-on experiments you can run today.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:24.600
First up, you can watch an AI think in real time with a free demo built by Anthropic and Neuronpedia.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:32.380
It uses the same JSpace mapping, so you see colored highlights move across different parts of the model as it processes prompts.

00:03:33.380 --> 00:03:40.220
Go to the Neuronpedia site, choose one of the example prompts, and simply watch the activity shift while the answer builds.

00:03:40.940 --> 00:03:45.000
No sign-up or special setup is required, and it works right in your browser.

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:53.280
Second, Google recently changed its settings so it can train AI on more of your searches and chats unless you turn that off.

00:03:54.020 --> 00:03:59.220
The fix takes only a minute and keeps your data private while you keep using the service normally.

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:08.580
Open your Google account settings on your phone or computer, look for the section about data sharing for AI improvement and toggle the option off.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:13.300
That single change stops the training use without affecting your everyday searches.

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:23.020
In QuickBits, Tencent released an open source model named High3 that is huge on paper yet only uses a small slice of itself for each task.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:29.680
It reportedly performs as well as models two to five times larger while making half as many mistakes.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:38.280
You can download and test it if your computer has the needed hardware, giving you a chance to explore a powerful system without paying for access.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:45.780
Also, a fully computer-generated character named Tilly Norwood is reportedly getting her own feature length film.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:51.820
It is an early hint at how AI-created performers could appear in stories we watch on-screen.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:05:00.360
And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool, go play with it. Curiosity is how every expert started.

